/// \defgroup grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree EAC RefPack codec and BTree encoder
/// \brief EA Canada's RefPack (LZ77) decoder and encoder and its BTree
///        (byte-pair) encoder, two of the codecs behind CompressionManager.
///
/// EA Canada's CODEX library gives every codec the same five C entry
/// points (about, is, size, decode and encode), declared per codec in a
/// small header such as refcodex.h or btreecodex.h on top of codex.h. This
/// group covers the RefPack functions REF_is, REF_size, REF_decode and
/// REF_encode, and BTREE_encode with its helpers. REF_about and
/// BTREE_about are declared but have no body in the BFME 2 tree. The BTree
/// decoder in btreedecode.cpp is not covered here, and the Huffman codec has
/// its own group, \ref grp_w1_eac_huffman. \ref sub_wwlib_thirdparty
/// describes the libraries around them.
///
/// \par Callers
/// At the time of writing, CompressionManager is the only named direct
/// caller found in BFME 2 for REF_decode, REF_encode and BTREE_encode;
/// REF_is and REF_size have no named caller, and the remaining functions are
/// called only from inside their own codec files. The search does not see
/// calls from unnamed code or through function-pointer tables.
/// CompressionManager::compressData writes an 8-byte header (a four-byte
/// tag, "EAR" for RefPack or "EAB" for BTree with a trailing NUL, then the
/// uncompressed length as a native int) and passes the rest of the buffer
/// to REF_encode or BTREE_encode. CompressionManager::decompressData
/// recognises the tag through CompressionManager::getCompressionType and
/// passes the bytes after the header to REF_decode or the BTree decoder.
/// At the time of writing no named BFME 2 caller of compressData or
/// decompressData is identified, so which BFME 2 data is stored compressed
/// is not established. Zero Hour: WorldBuilder saves a map with RefPack
/// unless the map's settings choose no compression (Zero Hour's
/// CompressionType enum ends the selectable range, COMPRESSION_MIN to
/// COMPRESSION_MAX, at COMPRESSION_REFPACK), and CachedFileInputStream
/// expands compressed chunk files such as maps when it opens them.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// The code is Zero Hour's EAC source, which BFME 1 also carries. BFME 2's
/// copies differ in build details:
/// - The encoders call the imported C runtime malloc and free where Zero
///   Hour writes gimex.h's galloc and gfree (in Zero Hour's gimex.h, macros
///   for those two functions).
/// - refdecode.cpp carries its own copy of the gimex.h reader ggetm.
/// - refencode.cpp defines its own file-local copy of the big-endian byte
///   writer that Zero Hour takes from gimex.h as the inline gputm, and
///   REF_encode writes its header through it. In the retail build those
///   writes are inlined into REF_encode; the ledger also matches a separate
///   compiled copy of the writer, for which no direct caller has been found.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing here reads game data definitions, and the formats' limits are
///   fixed: RefPack copies at most 1,028 bytes from at most 131,072 bytes
///   back, and the BTree header holds a 24-bit length.
/// - If BFME 2 data reaches CompressionManager (not yet established), a
///   RefPack-tagged stream must be in the format REF_decode reads, and
///   because the decoders check no bounds, a damaged stream can crash
///   instead of failing cleanly.
/// - BFME 1's RAMFile::openFromArchive expands BIG archive entries that
///   start with a 0x15FB header (the signature, then a 4-byte length) using
///   its own RefPack decoder. REF_is rejects that signature, and REF_decode,
///   which checks no signature, would misread its header: 0x15FB has bit
///   0x0100 set and bit 0x8000 clear, so REF_decode skips three bytes and
///   reads a 3-byte length, while BFME 1 reads a 4-byte length right after
///   the signature.
/// - The matched BFME 2 RAMFile::openFromArchive is Zero Hour's version,
///   which expands nothing. BFME 2's StreamingArchiveFile::openFromArchive
///   compares an entry's first two bytes with 0x15fb and, on a match,
///   raises a release-build crash report, 'Streaming from a compressed
///   archive file is not supported', in CrashDone's fatal mode (see
///   \ref grp_w1_debug_lib), so the game is expected to exit; it never
///   decodes the entry. It reads the two
///   bytes as a native little-endian value, the opposite order to the
///   big-endian 0x15FB test in BFME 1's RAMFile::openFromArchive. Whether
///   any other BFME 2 path unpacks archive entries is not established.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The codecs are written in a plain C style and compiled as C++, with C
///   linkage for the public entry points. They make no platform calls: only
///   malloc, free, memcpy and memset. They read and write multi-byte fields
///   one byte at a time, so they do not depend on the host byte order. The
///   length in the CompressionManager header is a native int, which does.
/// - Neither encoder knows the size of its output buffer, and both can
///   write more bytes than they were given (see REF_encode and
///   BTREE_encode).


/// \file Compression/EAC/codex.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree
/// \brief Common declarations of EA Canada's CODEX codec library,
///        version 2.00.
///
/// Defines CODEXABOUT, a codec's description of itself (decode, encode and
/// 32-bit size support flags, version and type names); QFUNCTIONS, a table
/// of one codec's five entry points; and GCALL, the calling convention every
/// entry point uses (stdcall under MSVC). The codec headers, such as
/// refcodex.h and btreecodex.h, declare their functions in this shape. The
/// header also pulls in gimex.h for its memory helpers.


/// \file Compression/EAC/refcodex.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree
/// \brief Public C interface of the RefPack codec: REF_is, REF_size,
///        REF_decode and REF_encode.
///
/// Include codex.h first. Compiled as C++, the last argument of REF_decode
/// and REF_encode defaults to null.
///
/// It also declares REF_about, whose Zero Hour body (refabout.cpp) is not
/// part of the BFME 2 tree.


/// \file Compression/EAC/btreecodex.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree
/// \brief Public C interface of the BTree codec: BTREE_is, BTREE_size and
///        BTREE_decode (btreedecode.cpp) and BTREE_encode (btreeencode.cpp).
///
/// Include codex.h first. Compiled as C++, the last argument of
/// BTREE_decode and BTREE_encode defaults to null.
///
/// It also declares BTREE_about, whose Zero Hour body (btreeabout.cpp) is
/// not part of the BFME 2 tree.


/// \file Compression/EAC/refdecode.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree
/// \brief RefPack decoder: REF_is, REF_size and REF_decode.
///
/// Zero Hour's refdecode.cpp with one addition: a file-local copy of
/// ggetm, the helper REF_is and REF_size read header fields with. It
/// returns 1, 2, 3 or 4 bytes as a big-endian unsigned number, and 0 for any
/// other count; it checks neither the pointer nor the buffer length.
///
/// Zero Hour takes ggetm from gimex.h, EA's graphics import and export
/// header, as a static inline function. This file defines its own copy so
/// that the compiled body matches retail, which keeps one out-of-line copy.
/// huffdecode.cpp defines an identical copy, and the ledger maps both to
/// that single retail body, which REF_size and HUFF_size call.


/// \file Compression/EAC/refencode.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree
/// \brief RefPack encoder: REF_encode and its internal helpers refcompress
///        and matchlen.
///
/// The file also holds its own copy of the big-endian byte writer that
/// REF_encode writes its header with; \ref grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree
/// describes it under Differences from Zero Hour.


/// \file Compression/EAC/btreeencode.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree
/// \brief BTree encoder: BTREE_encode and the file-local helpers that pick
///        byte pairs, rewrite the data and pack the output.
///
/// Each encode keeps its state in a BTreeEncodeContext on BTREE_encode's
/// stack, in BTREE_treepack's local tables and in heap buffers that
/// BTREE_treepack allocates; the file has no global variables.


/// \class BTREEMemStruct
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree
/// \brief A byte buffer and a length: how the BTree encoder describes its
///        input and its output.
///
/// BTREE_encode builds two on its stack, one for the source and one for the
/// destination. The input's length is the source size. The output's length
/// is reset to zero by BTREE_compressfile and advanced by BTREE_writebits
/// for every byte written, so it ends as the encoded size. Neither buffer is
/// owned: both belong to BTREE_encode's caller, and nothing records the
/// output buffer's capacity.


/// \class BTreeEncodeContext
/// \ingroup grp_w1_eac_refpack_btree
/// \brief Working state of one BTree encode: the bit writer, the pair
///        tables and the two work buffers.
///
/// BTREE_encode keeps one on its stack for a single call and passes it to
/// BTREE_compressfile, BTREE_treepack, BTREE_joinnodes and BTREE_writebits.
/// BTREE_treepack frees the work buffers before returning, except after an
/// allocation failure, when they leak.
///
/// Fields, by role:
/// - Bit writer: workpattern holds bits not yet written and packbits counts
///   them, masks holds the low-bit masks for 0 to 16 bits, and plen counts
///   bytes written. BTREE_compressfile initialises them.
/// - Data: buffer and bufptr bound the data being packed and ulen is its
///   length. BTREE_treepack copies the caller's input into buf1 first, so
///   the input is never modified.
/// - Work buffers: buf1 and buf2, each one and a half times the input plus
///   16,384 bytes. BTREE_joinnodes copies from one to the other on every
///   pass, and bufbase and bufend mark the current contents.
/// - Code tables, indexed by byte value: clueq tells BTREE_joinnodes how to
///   treat each byte, and right and join, indexed by a pair's first byte,
///   give its second byte and the code that replaces the pair.


/// \fn bool REF_is(const void *compresseddata)
/// \brief Tells whether a buffer starts with a RefPack stream signature.
///
/// Reads the first two bytes big-endian and accepts the four RefPack
/// signatures 0x10FB, 0x11FB, 0x90FB and 0x91FB (REF_decode explains the
/// flag bits). Nothing else is checked, so a match does not prove that the
/// rest of the stream is well formed. CompressionManager recognises RefPack
/// by its own "EAR" tag and does not call this, and no other named direct
/// caller has been found so far (see the group's Callers notes).
///
/// \param compresseddata Start of the candidate stream. At least two bytes
///                       must be readable; null is not checked.
/// \return true for one of the four signatures, false otherwise.
/// \see REF_size, REF_decode


/// \fn int REF_size(const void *compresseddata)
/// \brief Reads the uncompressed length from a RefPack header without
///        decoding.
///
/// Use it to size the output buffer before REF_decode. Bit 0x8000 of the
/// signature selects 4-byte size fields instead of 3-byte ones, and when
/// bit 0x0100 is set an extra size field of the same width comes first and
/// is skipped (the source's comment calls it ulen; its meaning is not used
/// here). The fields are read with the file's ggetm helper.
/// CompressionManager takes the length from its own header instead
/// (CompressionManager::getUncompressedSize), and no other named direct
/// caller has been found so far (see the group's Callers notes).
///
/// \param compresseddata Start of the stream at its signature. Neither null
///                       nor the signature is checked.
/// \return The uncompressed length in bytes, the same value REF_decode
///         returns.
/// \see REF_is, REF_decode


/// \fn int REF_decode(void *dest, const void *compresseddata, int *compressedsize)
/// \brief Expands one EA RefPack stream into a caller-supplied buffer.
///
/// RefPack is the byte-oriented LZ77 format of EA Canada's codec library.
/// CompressionManager::decompressData calls this when the 8-byte
/// CompressionManager header carries the RefPack tag, passing the bytes
/// after that header. It is the only named direct caller found so far, and
/// decompressData's own callers are not yet identified (see the group's
/// Callers notes). Zero Hour: one route is CachedFileInputStream, whose
/// open function unpacks compressed chunk files such as maps before
/// DataChunkInput parses them. Use REF_is to recognise a stream and REF_size
/// to learn how large the output must be.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The stream starts with a big-endian 16-bit signature, normally 0x10FB,
/// 0x11FB, 0x90FB or 0x91FB; only two of its bits are read. Bit 0x8000
/// selects 4-byte size fields instead of 3-byte ones; bit 0x0100 means an
/// extra size field of the same width comes first and is skipped (the
/// source's comment calls it ulen; its meaning is not used here). The next
/// size field is the uncompressed length, which is the return value. The
/// body is a sequence of commands chosen by the top bits of their first
/// byte:
/// - 0xxxxxxx (2 bytes): 0-3 literals, then copy 3-10 bytes from up to
///   1,024 bytes back.
/// - 10xxxxxx (3 bytes): 0-3 literals, then copy 4-67 bytes from up to
///   16,384 bytes back.
/// - 110xxxxx (4 bytes): 0-3 literals, then copy 5-1,028 bytes from up to
///   131,072 bytes back.
/// - 0xE0-0xFB: a run of 4-112 literals, in steps of four.
/// - 0xFC-0xFF: 0-3 final literals, then the end of the stream.
///
/// Back-references are copied one byte at a time, so a reference that
/// overlaps the bytes being written repeats them, which is how runs are
/// encoded.
///
/// \param dest           Output buffer. It must hold at least REF_size
///                       bytes; nothing here checks its size.
/// \param compresseddata Start of the RefPack stream at its signature. A
///                       null pointer decodes nothing and returns 0.
/// \param compressedsize Optional. Receives the number of input bytes
///                       consumed. Its value on entry is ignored.
/// \return The uncompressed length from the stream header. The function
///         does not check that the commands produced exactly that many
///         bytes.
///
/// \warning Malformed input can read and write past either buffer. Only
///          pass streams from trusted data.
/// \note decompressData passes the remaining input length in
///       compressedsize, but this function never reads that value as a
///       limit.
/// \par Modding
/// REF_decode does not check the signature: it reads only bits 0x8000 and
/// 0x0100 and decodes whatever follows as one of the four forms REF_is
/// accepts, so check a stream with REF_is first. The encoder writes the
/// 4-byte-size form 0x90FB only for inputs of 16 MiB or more. The 0x15FB
/// variant that BFME 1's RAMFile::openFromArchive expands (the signature,
/// then a 4-byte length) is misread here: REF_decode skips three bytes and
/// takes a 3-byte length from the wrong place. Zero Hour: WorldBuilder
/// saves a map with RefPack unless the map's settings choose no compression
/// (the only other choice Zero Hour's enum allows there), and a
/// RefPack-compressed map reaches the game through this function.
///
/// \see REF_is, REF_size, REF_encode, CompressionManager::decompressData


/// \fn int REF_encode(void *compresseddata, const void *source, int sourcesize, int *opts)
/// \brief Compresses a buffer into a RefPack stream.
///
/// CompressionManager::compressData calls it for COMPRESSION_REFPACK after
/// writing its own 8-byte header with the "EAR" tag. That is the only named
/// direct caller found so far; see the group's Callers notes for
/// compressData's own callers.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Writes the signature and length, then hands the input to refcompress.
/// - Inputs smaller than 16 MiB get signature 0x10FB and a 3-byte length;
///   inputs of 16 MiB or more get 0x90FB and a 4-byte length.
/// - The 0x11FB and 0x91FB variants, with the extra leading size field,
///   are never written.
/// - refcompress always runs in its thorough mode, with its fixed
///   131,071-byte window.
///
/// \param compresseddata Output buffer. Nothing checks its size; see the
///                       warning.
/// \param source         Bytes to compress. Not modified.
/// \param sourcesize     Number of bytes in source.
/// \param opts           Ignored. CompressionManager passes null.
/// \return Bytes written: the 5- or 6-byte header plus the compressed body.
///
/// \warning The output can be larger than the input. Data without repeats
///          costs one command byte per 112 literals, plus the header and the
///          end command. CompressionManager::getMaxCompressedSize allows only
///          the input length plus CompressionManager's own 8-byte header,
///          which is too small for such data.
/// \warning If refcompress cannot allocate its tables, the return value is
///          just the header length, which a caller cannot tell from
///          success.
/// \see REF_decode, refcompress, CompressionManager::compressData


/// \fn int refcompress(unsigned char *from, int len, unsigned char *dest, int maxback, int quick)
/// \brief Greedy LZ77 match finder that writes the RefPack command stream
///        for REF_encode.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Candidate matches come from a hash of the next three bytes (65,536
///   buckets) and a 131,072-entry chain linking earlier positions with the
///   same hash. Both tables are allocated here (768 KiB with a 32-bit int)
///   and freed before returning.
/// - At each position the whole chain inside the window is walked; a
///   candidate is measured with matchlen only if it agrees with the current
///   position at the current best length, so only candidates that could
///   beat the best match are measured. The match that saves the most bytes,
///   after the size of its command, wins. A command costs 2 bytes for an
///   offset under 1,024 and a length up to 10, 3 bytes for an offset under
///   16,384 and a length up to 67, and 4 bytes otherwise, so a match must
///   be at least 3 bytes long within 1,024 bytes, 4 within 16,384 bytes and
///   5 farther back. The walk ends early only on a 1,028-byte match.
///   Otherwise every chain entry inside the window is examined, so input
///   with many repeated short byte sequences (long hash chains whose
///   matches stay shorter than 1,028 bytes) can be slow to compress.
/// - A match is used only while at least eight input bytes remain, and is
///   never longer than the remaining input less four bytes, so the last
///   four input bytes are always stored as literals.
/// - Unmatched bytes accumulate. Before each match they go out in literal
///   commands of up to 112 bytes, whole groups of four; the last 0-3 travel
///   in the match command, or in the end command at the end of the input.
/// - The choice is greedy: there is no look-ahead to a better match at the
///   next position.
///
/// \param from    Input bytes.
/// \param len     Input length in bytes.
/// \param dest    Output buffer, just after the header REF_encode wrote.
/// \param maxback Clamped to 131,071 but otherwise unused: the search
///                window is always 131,071 bytes.
/// \param quick   Nonzero hashes only the first position of each match,
///                which is faster but leaves later searches fewer
///                candidates. REF_encode passes 0.
/// \return Bytes written to dest, or 0 if a table could not be allocated.
///
/// \warning If the second table cannot be allocated, the first one is not
///          freed.
/// \see REF_encode, matchlen, REF_decode


/// \fn unsigned int matchlen(unsigned char *s, unsigned char *d, unsigned int maxmatch)
/// \brief Counts the leading bytes two positions have in common, up to a
///        limit.
///
/// \param s        Current input position.
/// \param d        Earlier candidate position.
/// \param maxmatch Upper bound on the count. refcompress passes the smaller
///                 of 1,028 and the remaining input less four bytes.
/// \return Number of equal leading bytes, at most maxmatch.
///
/// \note In the retail build refcompress has this loop inlined. The ledger
///       also matches a separate compiled copy of matchlen, for which no
///       direct caller has been found.
/// \see refcompress


/// \fn int BTREE_encode(void *compresseddata, const void *source, int sourcesize, int *opts)
/// \brief Compresses a buffer with EA's BTree byte-pair coder.
///
/// CompressionManager::compressData calls it for COMPRESSION_BTREE after
/// writing its own 8-byte header with the "EAB" tag. That is the only named
/// direct caller found so far; see the group's Callers notes for
/// compressData's own callers.
/// The matching decoder is BTREE_decode in btreedecode.cpp.
///
/// BTree replaces frequent pairs of adjacent bytes with byte values the data
/// can spare, pass after pass, so that one code can stand for a long string:
/// each code expands to two bytes, either of which can itself be a code,
/// which forms a binary tree. There is no window and there are no
/// back-references. A code costs one escape for every time its byte value
/// occurs in the input, so the coder gains most when some byte values are
/// rare or unused, as in text.
///
/// \par Stream format
/// Every field is a whole number of bytes, multi-byte fields big-endian:
/// - signature 0x46FB and the 3-byte uncompressed length;
/// - the escape byte (the "clue") and the number of pair codes;
/// - three bytes per pair code: the code, its first byte and its second
///   byte;
/// - the packed data, in which a pair code expands to its two bytes, the
///   clue followed by a nonzero byte stands for that byte, and any other
///   byte stands for itself;
/// - the clue followed by 0, which ends the stream.
///
/// \param compresseddata Output buffer. Nothing checks its size; see the
///                       warning.
/// \param source         Bytes to compress. Copied first, never modified.
/// \param sourcesize     Number of bytes in source.
/// \param opts           Optional. When it is not null and opts[0] is
///                       nonzero, byte values 0-31 are never paired or used
///                       as codes, which suits text. CompressionManager
///                       passes null.
/// \return Bytes written, including the 5-byte header.
///
/// \warning The length field holds 24 bits, so an input of 16 MiB or more
///          is recorded with a wrong length.
/// \warning The output can be larger than the input. With nothing to join
///          it is the input plus 9 bytes, plus one escape for every
///          occurrence of the clue, which is more than
///          CompressionManager::getMaxCompressedSize allows. If a work
///          buffer cannot be allocated, only the header is written and its
///          length returned, which a caller cannot tell from success.
/// \see BTREE_compressfile, BTREE_treepack, CompressionManager::compressData


/// \fn int BTREE_compressfile(struct BTreeEncodeContext *EC, struct BTREEMemStruct *infile, struct BTREEMemStruct *outfile, int ulen, int zerosuppress)
/// \brief Sets up the encode context, writes the stream header and runs
///        BTREE_treepack.
///
/// Fixes the tuning BTREE_encode gets: at most 256 joining passes, at most
/// 32 new pair codes per pass, and BTREE_treepack's default cut-off for
/// candidate pairs.
///
/// When ulen equals the input length, which is always the case from
/// BTREE_encode, the header is 0x46FB and a 3-byte length. Otherwise it is
/// 0x47FB, ulen and then the input length, a form the source calls a
/// composite header; the decoder skips the first length and uses the
/// second.
///
/// \param EC           Context to initialise. Owned by the caller.
/// \param infile       Input buffer and length.
/// \param outfile      Output buffer. Its length is reset to 0 and grows
///                     as bytes are written.
/// \param ulen         Length recorded in the header; see above.
/// \param zerosuppress Nonzero keeps byte values 0-31 out of pair coding.
/// \return Bytes written to outfile.
/// \see BTREE_encode, BTREE_treepack, BTREE_writebits


/// \fn void BTREE_treepack(struct BTreeEncodeContext *EC, struct BTREEMemStruct *dest, unsigned int passes, unsigned int multimax, unsigned int quick, int zerosuppress)
/// \brief Chooses the pair codes, rewrites the data with them and writes
///        everything after the stream header.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. Allocates a table of 65,536 16-bit pair counts and the two work
///    buffers, and copies the input into the first buffer.
/// 2. Counts each byte value in the input. The least frequent value (on a
///    tie, the higher one) becomes the clue, the escape byte, and any
///    occurrences of it in the data are escaped. Byte 0's count is replaced
///    by a fixed 32,000, so it is picked as the clue or as a code only when
///    every other candidate occurs more than 32,000 times (ties go to the
///    higher byte value). The end marker (the
///    clue followed by 0) is unambiguous only while 0 is neither the clue
///    nor a code, which this makes unlikely but does not guarantee for very
///    large inputs.
///    Bytes seen three times or fewer start out ineligible for pairing.
/// 3. Each pass counts the adjacent byte pairs (BTREE_clearcount,
///    BTREE_adjcount) and ranks the most frequent pairs of eligible bytes
///    (BTREE_findbest). Walking that list, it joins a pair when neither byte
///    has been used yet in this pass and a free code exists whose cost, three
///    bytes of tree plus one escape per occurrence of the code in the
///    original input, is below the pair's count. Free codes are taken least
///    frequent first. The walk stops at the end of the list, at the first
///    eligible pair that does not pay for itself or finds no free code, or
///    after multimax joins.
/// 4. BTREE_joinnodes then rewrites the data. A byte that has been part of a
///    pair is never used as a code afterwards. Bytes used in a pass,
///    including its new codes, become eligible for pairing again in the
///    next pass, which is how the tree grows.
///    Passes end when one joins nothing or passes runs out.
/// 5. Writes the clue, the number of codes, each code's three-byte entry,
///    the packed data and the end marker through BTREE_writebits, and frees
///    the buffers.
///
/// \param EC           Encode context; BTREE_compressfile has set the input
///                     and the bit writer.
/// \param dest         Output buffer, after the header.
/// \param passes       Maximum number of joining passes.
/// \param multimax     Maximum number of pairs joined in one pass.
/// \param quick        Nonzero sets the cut-off ratio BTREE_findbest uses;
///                     0 means a ratio of 2. BTREE_compressfile passes 0.
/// \param zerosuppress Nonzero keeps byte values 0-31 from being paired or
///                     used as codes.
///
/// \warning On an allocation failure it returns without writing anything
///          and without freeing the buffers it already allocated.
/// \see BTREE_compressfile, BTREE_findbest, BTREE_joinnodes


/// \fn unsigned int BTREE_findbest(BTREEWORD *countptr, unsigned char *tryq, unsigned int *bestn, unsigned int *bestval, int ratio)
/// \brief Ranks the most frequent eligible byte pairs for one joining pass.
///
/// Scans the pair counts whose first and second bytes are both eligible and
/// keeps up to 47 pairs sorted by count, most frequent first, from index 1.
/// The acceptance threshold starts at four occurrences, then follows the
/// leading pair's count divided by ratio, or the smallest listed count once
/// the list is full. Listed pairs that fall below the leader's count divided
/// by ratio are dropped.
///
/// \param countptr The 256 by 256 table of pair counts, indexed by first
///                 byte times 256 plus second byte.
/// \param tryq     Eligibility per byte value; only nonzero entries are
///                 scanned.
/// \param bestn    [out] Listed pairs, as first byte times 256 plus second
///                 byte.
/// \param bestval  [in,out] Their counts. Entry 0 must hold a sentinel larger
///                 than any count; BTREE_treepack stores the largest
///                 unsigned value there.
/// \param ratio    Divisor for the cut-off, 2 by default.
/// \return One more than the number of pairs listed.
/// \see BTREE_treepack, BTREE_adjcount


/// \fn void BTREE_joinnodes(struct BTreeEncodeContext *EC, unsigned char *cluep, unsigned char *rightp, unsigned char *joinp, unsigned int clue)
/// \brief Rewrites the data once, replacing this pass's pairs with their
///        codes and escaping bytes that must not be read as codes.
///
/// Copies the current work buffer into the other one, steered by the
/// per-byte table cluep:
/// - 0: copy the byte.
/// - 1: first byte of a pair being joined. If the next byte is its partner
///   in rightp, write the code from joinp in place of both.
/// - 2: the clue. Copy it and the byte after it unchanged, keeping an
///   earlier escape intact.
/// - 3: a byte that is now a code. Write the clue in front of it.
///
/// The clue is also stored just past the end of the data as a sentinel that
/// stops the copy loop. On return the context's current buffer and end
/// point at the new contents; the end excludes the sentinel.
///
/// \param EC     Encode context holding the two work buffers.
/// \param cluep  Action per byte value, as listed above.
/// \param rightp Second byte of the pair for each first byte.
/// \param joinp  Code replacing the pair for each first byte.
/// \param clue   The escape byte.
/// \see BTREE_treepack


/// \fn void BTREE_adjcount(unsigned char *s, unsigned char *bend, BTREEWORD *count)
/// \brief Counts every pair of adjacent bytes in a buffer.
///
/// Adds one to count at first byte times 256 plus second byte for each
/// adjacent pair, in a loop unrolled sixteen times. Rows of ineligible bytes
/// are counted too, but BTREE_findbest never reads them.
///
/// \param s     First byte of the data.
/// \param bend  One past the last byte.
/// \param count [in,out] The 256 by 256 table of pair counts.
///
/// \note The counts are signed 16-bit values. A pair seen more than 32,767
///       times in one pass wraps, and BTREE_findbest then ignores or
///       undercounts it, so very large inputs can pack less well than they
///       could.
/// \see BTREE_clearcount, BTREE_findbest


/// \fn void BTREE_clearcount(unsigned char *tryq, BTREEWORD *countbuf)
/// \brief Zeroes the pair-count rows of the eligible bytes before a pass.
///
/// For every byte value whose tryq entry is nonzero it sets the entry back
/// to 1 and clears that byte's row of 256 counts; other rows are left
/// alone. BTREE_treepack marks the bytes it uses in a pass, including the
/// new codes, with 2, so resetting the entry to 1 makes them eligible for
/// pairing again. Each row is cleared as 128 ints,
/// which assumes an int is twice the size of the 16-bit count.
///
/// \param tryq     [in,out] Eligibility per byte value.
/// \param countbuf [out] The 256 by 256 table of pair counts.
/// \see BTREE_adjcount, BTREE_treepack


/// \fn void BTREE_writebits(struct BTreeEncodeContext *EC, struct BTREEMemStruct *dest, unsigned int bitpattern, unsigned int len)
/// \brief Appends the low bits of a value to the output, most significant
///        bit first.
///
/// Bits collect in the context until a whole byte is ready; each finished
/// byte is stored at the output's current length, which advances together
/// with the context's byte count. Values longer than 16 bits are written as
/// two calls, high part first. Every field the encoder writes is a whole
/// number of bytes, so the final 7-bit flush in BTREE_treepack writes
/// nothing.
///
/// \param EC         Encode context; its masks must be set up
///                   (BTREE_compressfile does that).
/// \param dest       Output buffer and current length.
/// \param bitpattern Value whose low len bits are written.
/// \param len        Number of bits, up to 32.
/// \see BTREE_compressfile, BTREE_treepack
